  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 8.459pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(143.399pt, 310.919pt, " Sketch of a 6-legged actuator for multiaxial strain tuning of a QD embedded in a GaAs nanomembrane ")
  #line-at(93.779pt, 320.699pt, "bonded on its top surface. Six independent voltages (V")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 5.639pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(279.3pt, 321.18pt, "1")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 8.459pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(282.12pt, 320.7pt, "- V")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 5.639pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(293.16pt, 321.18pt, "6")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 8.459pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(295.98pt, 320.7pt, ") are applied to the individual legs at the bottom of the actuator ")
  #line-at(93.781pt, 330.42pt, "by contacting each of the legs on a chip carrier. ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.32pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(93.78pt, 351.3pt, "This work covers the development of the process steps for thinning (Fig. 1a), contact layer structuring ")
  #line-at(93.781pt, 363.18pt, "(Fig. 1c), and UV cutting (Fig. 1d). In addition to the development of the laser processes, the ")
  #line-at(93.78pt, 375.12pt, "influence of the metal layer thickness on the conductivity over the non-planar areas is explored (Fig. ")
  #line-at(93.78pt, 387.06pt, "1b). The final integration of the actuator (see  Fig. 1e) is identical to previous work, with the ")
  #line-at(93.781pt, 398.88pt, "difference that local thinning creates a space between the PMN-PT and the chip carrier in the active ")
  #line-at(93.781pt, 410.82pt, "area. This is an improvement as the free movement of the individual arms is always guaranteed.  ")
  #line-at(93.781pt, 430.201pt, "Compared to actuators in previous work [22], the experimental design was miniaturized in terms of ")
  #line-at(93.781pt, 442.141pt, "lateral diameter and cutting width. It consists of two symmetrical sides with two cantilevers (arms) ")
  #line-at(93.781pt, 454.021pt, "and a center bar, a configuration which enables the individual manipulation of strain in two regions in ")
  #line-at(93.781pt, 465.961pt, "close vicinity to each other. ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 15.057pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(93.78pt, 512.88pt, "2. Experimental")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.32pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(93.78pt, 525.84pt, "The material used for all experiments was x2b from TRS ceramics with a thickness of 300 µm. A ")
  #line-at(93.78pt, 537.72pt, "femtosecond laser source (Spirit")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 6.598pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(227.4pt, 533.52pt, "(R)")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.32pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(236.22pt, 537.72pt, " 1040-HE from Spectra Physics) with a pulse duration of 330 fs ")
  #line-at(93.78pt, 549.66pt, "(260 fs @ SH) was used for the ablation experiments. The laser is integrated in a micromachining ")
  #line-at(93.779pt, 561.54pt, "system “microstructVario” from 3D Micromac. For most experiments the second harmonic at a ")
  #line-at(93.779pt, 573.48pt, "wavelength of 523 nm was used. The beam was focused and manipulated using a galvo scanner with a ")
  #line-at(93.779pt, 585.36pt, "lens of f=170 mm focal length. The cutting experiments were performed with the third harmonic (UV) ")
  #line-at(93.779pt, 597.24pt, "wavelength at 348 nm using a f=160 mm lens. The beam radius was determined with the method of ")
  #line-at(93.779pt, 609.12pt, "Liu et al. [23] and was 11 µm for the 523 nm wavelength and 6 µm for the UV wavelength, ")
  #line-at(93.779pt, 621.06pt, "respectively. A repetition rate of 100 kHz was used for all thinning and layer structuring experiments. ")
  #line-at(93.779pt, 632.94pt, "The UV cutting experiments were performed with a repetition rate of 250 kHz. The substrates were ")
  #line-at(93.779pt, 644.88pt, "coated using vacuum evaporation with an e-gun. The machine used was a Balzers BAK 550 with ESQ ")
  #line-at(93.779pt, 656.701pt, "110 e-gun. The source material was held in a water-cooled graphite crucible. The profile ")
  #line-at(93.779pt, 668.641pt, "measurements of cavities were done with a Keyence VK-X250 Laser scanning microscope. ")
]

// arXiv source page 4
#original-page(612pt, 792pt, (image("graphics/page-04.png", width: 293.5pt), 159.5pt, 250.5pt))[
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 15.057pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(93.78pt, 81.78pt, "3. Results ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12.237pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(93.78pt, 98.34pt, "3.1 Generation of cavities with freely adjustable sidewall angles ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.32pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(93.78pt, 110.7pt, "As the first step of the actuator fabrication process, the PMN-PT substrate with a thickness of 300 µm ")
  #line-at(93.78pt, 122.58pt, "has to be thinned down to a residual thickness of 100 microns within the area of a circle (see Fig. 1b). ")
  #line-at(93.78pt, 134.52pt, "Thinning is realized by scanning the laser along parallel lines with a distance of 5 microns resulting in ")
  #line-at(93.781pt, 146.34pt, "a pattern which is repeated until the final depth is reached. In order to achieve a uniform removal with ")
  #line-at(93.781pt, 158.28pt, "a minimal surface roughness, the scanning pattern is rotated by a random value after each repetition. ")
  #line-at(93.781pt, 170.16pt, "This ensures that line intersections are statistically distributed over the surface. For a specific set of ")
  #line-at(93.782pt, 182.1pt, "laser and structuring parameters, the ablation depth per repetition was determined. For this, the depth ")
  #line-at(93.782pt, 193.98pt, "of a cavity was measured after a repetition number of N=200 using an optical microscope which ")
  #line-at(93.782pt, 205.86pt, "resulted in a value of 150 µm. Based on this measurement there is a focus shift of 150 µm/N=0.75 µm ")
  #line-at(93.782pt, 217.741pt, "applied after each repetition for all following experiments. This focus shift leads to a constant fluence ")
  #line-at(93.782pt, 229.681pt, "value on the substrate surface during the machining process and results in a simple linear relation ")
  #line-at(93.782pt, 241.561pt, "between ablation depth and number of repetitions which is shown in Fig. 2a.  ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 8.459pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(93.78pt, 497.4pt, "Fig. 2")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 8.459pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(114pt, 497.4pt, " Panel ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 8.459pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(137.039pt, 497.4pt, "(a)")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 8.459pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(146.939pt, 497.4pt, " shows the linear dependence of the depth removed per scan repetition. Measurements of the cavity profile ")
  #line-at(93.779pt, 507.12pt, "for different repetition values using a confocal microscope show indentations at the transition from the base to the side edge ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 8.459pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(93.78pt, 516.84pt, "(b)")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 8.459pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(104.1pt, 516.84pt, ". This becomes more and more pronounced as the number of repetitions increases. Panels ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 8.459pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(408.839pt, 516.84pt, "(c)")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 8.459pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(418.199pt, 516.84pt, " and ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 8.459pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(434.698pt, 516.84pt, "(d)")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 8.459pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(445.018pt, 516.84pt, " show SEM images ")
  #line-at(93.78pt, 526.56pt, "of the cavity at N=140 and 220, respectively. After 140 repetitions, the first signs of irregularities forming along the side ")
  #line-at(93.779pt, 536.34pt, "edge can already be seen. After 220, a clear ring with significant indentations has already formed. ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.32pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(93.78pt, 557.16pt, "In order to investigate the profile geometry, measurements using a Laser Scanning Microscope (LSM) ")
  #line-at(93.78pt, 569.04pt, "have been carried out, the results can be seen in panel b of Fig. 2. The cavities were measured after ")
  #line-at(93.78pt, 580.98pt, "machining with a selection of passes at N = 60, 140, 220 and 230. In all cases one can see that the ")
  #line-at(93.78pt, 592.86pt, "bottom plane is parallel to the substrate surface, indicating uniform removal. The transition from the ")
  #line-at(93.78pt, 604.8pt, "ground to the side edge forms a radius due to the Gaussian beam profile, as clearly visible after a ")
  #line-at(93.78pt, 616.68pt, "repetition number of N=60. An increased roughness in this area can already be recognized in the ")
  #line-at(93.781pt, 628.56pt, "measurement at N=140. After N=220 and higher, one can clearly see pits in this transition region. ")
  #line-at(93.781pt, 640.5pt, "Panels c and d show SEM images of the cavities created with N = 140 and N = 220, respectively. ")
  #line-at(93.781pt, 652.38pt, "They clearly show an increased roughness in the measurement and the beginning of the formation of ")
  #line-at(93.781pt, 664.32pt, "small holes along the sideline. By increasing the number of repetitions, these holes become even more ")
  #line-at(93.782pt, 676.2pt, "pronounced and form a recessed ring around the bottom surface. This effect is well known and has ")
  #line-at(93.782pt, 688.08pt, "already been reported [24], [25] . It is most likey caused by reflections at the side edge, as discussed ")
  #line-at(93.782pt, 699.961pt, "by Toenshoff and co-authors during their research on femtosecond silicon cutting [26]. The ")
  #line-at(93.782pt, 711.901pt, "reflections lead to an increased intensity in the edge area and the formation of the indentation. The ")
]

