  #line-at(93.78pt, 701.701pt, "as any existing deposits are constantly removed from the side edge by repetitions with a larger ")
]

// arXiv source page 6
#original-page(612pt, 792pt, (image("graphics/page-06.png", width: 425pt), 93.5pt, 299.5pt))[
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.32pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(93.78pt, 77.4pt, "diameter. In general, the RND and STL methods show similar morphologies with comparable surface ")
  #line-at(93.78pt, 89.34pt, "roughness. ")
  #line-at(93.78pt, 108.78pt, "With these two selected strategies, RND and STL, experiments were conducted where the inner ")
  #line-at(93.78pt, 120.66pt, "diameter was varied for each cavity. These span from 350 μm to 450 μm with steps of 20 μm, with the ")
  #line-at(93.78pt, 132.54pt, "aim to examine possible variations of the sidewall angle. This is particularly important for ")
  #line-at(93.78pt, 144.421pt, "applications of PMN-PT as actuators where electrical contacts are realised through vapor deposition ")
  #line-at(93.779pt, 156.361pt, "of a thin metal layer on the surface. Here, the sidewall angle and the surface quality have a decisive ")
  #line-at(93.779pt, 168.241pt, "impact on the conductance of the layer in these critical areas. ")
  #line-at(93.779pt, 187.681pt, "The cavity profiles realized in this way were again measured using the laser scanning microscope. ")
  #line-at(93.779pt, 199.561pt, "The results can be seen in the graphs of Fig. 4. In general, only a slight change in the sidewall angle ")
  #line-at(93.779pt, 211.501pt, "can be observed as a function of the inner diameter. The values deviate significantly from the ones ")
  #line-at(93.779pt, 223.321pt, "expected from geometric calculations and are comparable for both methods. A possible explanation ")
  #line-at(93.779pt, 235.261pt, "for the low variation in flank angles is that for low changes in diameter (less than 1 micron) the angles ")
  #line-at(93.779pt, 247.141pt, "and the profile are influenced predominantly by the gaussian profile of the beam rather than ")
  #line-at(93.779pt, 259.081pt, "geometrical parameters.  ")
  #line-at(93.779pt, 278.462pt, "A difference between the RDN and the STL method can be observed in the formation of the bottom ")
  #line-at(93.778pt, 290.402pt, "surface. ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 8.459pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(158.7pt, 450pt, "Fig. 4")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 8.459pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(178.98pt, 450pt, " Profiles of cavities with varied inner diameter. The angles show small variations ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.32pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(93.78pt, 470.88pt, "For parameters that lead to steeper edge angles, RND shows a slight indentation in the corner region, ")
  #line-at(93.78pt, 482.82pt, "whereas with the STL method, the entire bottom surface shows a uniform depth. However, with ")
  #line-at(93.781pt, 494.7pt, "smaller inner diameters, this method shows a significantly more curved bottom surface compared to ")
  #line-at(93.781pt, 506.64pt, "RND. ")
  #line-at(93.781pt, 526.08pt, "In order to further examine the possible variation in flank angles towards significantly lower values, ")
  #line-at(93.782pt, 537.96pt, "cavities were generated by applying the same parameters as in table 1 but with the chosen geometry ")
  #line-at(93.782pt, 549.84pt, "altered. Instead of using a circle, a rectangle with 500 microns in width and 2.5 mm in length was ")
  #line-at(93.782pt, 561.721pt, "chosen. The rectangle was then shifted by a fixed value from left to right for each of the 200 ")
  #line-at(93.782pt, 573.661pt, "repetitions. The values range from 0 to 10 µm, with a step of 1 micron. In total, 11 cavities were ")
  #line-at(93.781pt, 585.541pt, "generated with this method. By using this scheme, the left side resembled LTS, while the right side ")
  #line-at(93.781pt, 597.481pt, "resembled STL. Fig. 5 shows the results for the left and right side of 3 cavities with a shift value of 1, ")
  #line-at(93.781pt, 609.361pt, "2 and 5 microns, respectively. ")
]

