  #line-at(215.579pt, 699.66pt, "angles correspond for offset values higher than 4 µm. ")
]

// arXiv source page 8
#original-page(612pt, 792pt, (image("graphics/page-08.png", width: 306pt), 152.5pt, 505pt))[
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.32pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(93.78pt, 77.4pt, "The profile measurements demonstrate clearly that it is possible to generate cavities with a larger ")
  #line-at(93.78pt, 89.34pt, "variety in flank angles. It can also be seen that, if the pattern is moved constantly, an inclined plane of ")
  #line-at(93.78pt, 101.16pt, "the same angle is formed on both the left and right side. In addition, the resulting angle corresponds ")
  #line-at(93.78pt, 113.1pt, "exactly to the calculated value for pattern shift values higher than 4 µm. Above this value, the ")
  #line-at(93.78pt, 124.98pt, "influence of the Gaussian beam profile which leads to higher angles as expected is no longer ")
  #line-at(93.78pt, 136.92pt, "observable. This makes it possible to realize quasi-3D structures (limited to non-undercut geometries) ")
  #line-at(93.78pt, 148.8pt, "by laser thinning with adapted hatch layers. Since the geometry of the side wall in terms of shape and ")
  #line-at(93.78pt, 160.74pt, "angle is independent of the processing method, there is a clear difference in case of the bottom edge. ")
  #line-at(93.78pt, 172.62pt, "A rounded edge forms on the left which is of comparable dimensions as the laser beam. On the right, ")
  #line-at(93.78pt, 184.5pt, "there is a smooth transition from the bottom surface to the sidewall with a significantly higher radius. ")
  #line-at(93.78pt, 196.44pt, "We assume that the reason for the difference in radius lies in the different geometrical starting points. ")
  #line-at(93.78pt, 208.321pt, "When the rectangle is shifted to the right with each repetition, the ablation on the left side always ")
  #line-at(93.78pt, 220.261pt, "occurs on a flat surface, finally creating a sharp edge. Contrary to that, the ablation on the right side ")
  #line-at(93.78pt, 232.081pt, "always occurs over the end of the previous ablated surface and thus on an uneven surface condition. ")
  #line-at(93.78pt, 244.021pt, "This leads to ablation with reduced fluence in this area, which does not affect the final sidewall angle ")
  #line-at(93.779pt, 255.901pt, "but does lead to a rounding of the geometry at the bottom edge.  ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12.237pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(93.78pt, 296.52pt, "3.2 Realisation of conducting paths beyond laser thinned areas ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.32pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(93.78pt, 308.82pt, "In order to electrically control all, even thinned areas of the piezo, we must be able to apply a ")
  #line-at(93.78pt, 320.7pt, "continuous conductive layer over the previously processed surfaces. Since the non-even metallization ")
  #line-at(93.78pt, 332.64pt, "layer cannot be patterned easily by standard lithography methods, it is subsequently structured by ")
  #line-at(93.779pt, 344.52pt, "means of selective laser ablation to form individual areas for actuator control. To prepare the substrate ")
  #line-at(93.779pt, 356.46pt, "for evaporation tests, it was first thinned using the parameters for the circular hatch pattern with a ")
  #line-at(93.779pt, 368.34pt, "variation of the inner diameter. After a cleaning step with deionized water and subsequent treatment ")
  #line-at(93.779pt, 380.22pt, "in oxygen plasma the samples were coated with titanium in steps. During each coating process, 25 nm ")
